{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_65","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '413,475p' test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc | sed -n '1420,1438p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc | sed -n '1620,1643p'\"","aggregated_output":"  \"The receiver's attach!, counting attaches and recording each\n   reflection's source identity.\"\n  [t]\n  (let [base (s/attacher t) origin (atom {}) n (atom 0)]\n    {:attach! (fn [d]\n                (swap! n inc)\n                (let [r (base d)]\n                  (when-let [h (:dao.stream/handle r)] (swap! origin assoc h (:dao.stream/identity d)))\n                  r))\n     :origin origin :count n}))\n\n\n(defn handed-off\n  \"Lift, lower into a fresh receiver, relift at once, then satisfy through\n   the source's streams.\"\n  [world]\n  (let [surfaces (or (:surfaces world) #{:reader})\n        {:keys [toy peer serve!]} (served surfaces)\n        wire (rows/lift-task (:machine world) serve! (lift-opts))\n        corr (into {} (map (fn [[id _]] [id id])) @(:table peer))\n        receiver ((or (:receiver world) (constantly nil)))\n        {:keys [attach! origin] n :count} (recording-attacher toy)\n        recv (rows/lower-task (machine (merge {:attach-stream attach!} (:opts receiver))) wire attach!)\n        attaches @n\n        again (served surfaces)\n        relift (rows/lift-task recv (:serve! again) (lift-opts))\n        corr2 (into {} (map (fn [[id e]] [id (get @origin (:handle e))])) @(:table (:peer again)))\n        done (satisfy world recv #(drive-peers % [peer]))\n        _ (s/serve-mirror! peer)]\n    (when-let [f (:finish world)] (f))\n    (when-let [f (:finish receiver)] (f))\n    {:wire wire :recv recv :relift relift :corr corr :corr2 corr2 :attaches attaches\n     :halted (vm/halted? done) :value (normalize (vm/value done)) :trace @(:trace world)}))\n\n\n(defn root-task\n  [wire]\n  (rows/decode wire))\n\n\n(defn stream-identities\n  \"The distinct stream identities of the whole carrier, install children\n   included: one row table serves the tree.\"\n  [wire]\n  (into #{} (keep (fn [r] (when (= :stream (second r)) (nth r 2)))) (:yin.k/rows wire)))\n\n\n(defn rows-in\n  \"Every row node the decoded tree x reaches, once per path.\"\n  [x]\n  (cond\n    (and (map? x) (contains? x :kind) (contains? x :slots)) (cons x (rows-in (:slots x)))\n    (sequential? x) (mapcat rows-in x)\n    :else nil))\n\n\n(defn stream-occurrences\n  \"How many times the decoded tree reaches a :stream row.\"\n  [x]\n  (count (filter #(= :stream (:kind %)) (rows-in x))))\n\n\n(defn slot\n  1420\t                                (let [[bodies occurrences] (get scanned a)\n  1421\t                                      home (code/body-index bodies at)]\n  1422\t                                  (keep (fn [o]\n  1423\t                                          (when (and (encloses? bodies home (code/body-index bodies (:at o)))\n  1424\t                                                     (not (answered? (:name o) env)))\n  1425\t                                            (:name o)))\n  1426\t                                        occurrences))))\n  1427\t                      (:contexts @(:acc ctx)))]\n  1428\t    (when (seq missing)\n  1429\t      (outcome! :yin.k/unsatisfied nil {:yin.k/discovery :incomplete :yin.k/missing {:obligations missing}}))))\n  1430\t\n  1431\t\n  1432\t(declare lift-task*)\n  1433\t\n  1434\t\n  1435\t(defn- install-node\n  1436\t  [ctx mn inst opts]\n  1437\t  (let [{:keys [phase parent response]} inst]\n  1438\t    (when-not (contains? #{:running :parked} phase)\n  1620\t\n  1621\t(defn- check-dependencies!\n  1622\t  \"Step 9, dependency closure: requires and code agree, every segment has\n  1623\t   its trees (§6.1) and is a vector the receiver's kernel admits under its\n  1624\t   A, every closure names a closure of its code, every :prim is the\n  1625\t   receiver's.\"\n  1626\t  [recv task _role]\n  1627\t  (let [[_ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ requires code] (:slots task)]\n  1628\t    (when-not (and (map? requires) (= (set (:yin.k/segments requires)) (set (keys code))))\n  1629\t      (outcome! :yin.k/undecodable :requires {}))\n  1630\t    (let [trees (:yin.k/trees requires)\n  1631\t          missing (into #{} (remove #(seq (get trees %))) (keys code))]\n  1632\t      (when (seq missing)\n  1633\t        (outcome! :yin.k/unsatisfied nil {:yin.k/discovery :incomplete :yin.k/missing {:yin.k/trees missing}})))\n  1634\t    (doseq [[a v] (sort-by #(str (key %)) code)]\n  1635\t      (when-not (attaches? recv v a)\n  1636\t        (outcome! :yin.k/undecodable :segment {:yin.k/segment a})))\n  1637\t    (run! #(check-closure! code %) (rows-of task :closure true))\n  1638\t    (doseq [p (rows-of task :prim true)]\n  1639\t      (when-not (contains? (:primitives recv) (first (:slots p)))\n  1640\t        (outcome! :yin.k/unsatisfied nil {:yin.k/name (first (:slots p))})))))\n  1641\t\n  1642\t\n  1643\t(defn- check-custody!\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_66","type":"agent_message","text":"The skipped-read case is confirmed under the union-discharge mutant: it transports, carries no unrelated module store, and both source and receiver complete with 73. The missing-store case also accepts and attaches one stream before invocation fails, violating §2.6’s requirement that every `store-of` resolve to a carried module store. I’ll record both as merge blockers."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_67","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat > collab/1792300000000-review-6b-fix1.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md <<'EOF'\n# Slice 6b fix round 1 — independent re-review\n\nReviewed \"'`srvm-phase6b`, HEAD `a396f99699992e8dd0d17d45124054617e68c407`, against the previous findings, the fix report, and continuation design §§2.6, 3.3 and 7.2. The original F1–F5 cases are fixed, and the requested suites pass. Two additional dependency defects remain. No implementation or test edits remain; this findings file is the only permanent review addition.\n\n## Findings\n\n### F6 — Medium: discharge checks code that cannot run from the carried context\n\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1420–1426`\n\nThe context records its resume PC, but discharge uses that PC only to select a body. It then checks every free occurrence in that body and every enclosed body, including occurrences that are behind the resume PC and cannot be reached again. This can reject a valid continuation.\n\nIndependent evaluator-produced reproduction:\n\n```clojure\n{:type :if\n :test (s/lit false)\n :consequent (s/v '\"'ghost)\n :alternate (s/next-of (s/cursor-of {:type :stream/make :buffer 4}))}\n\"'```\n\nRun this to block, then add `:module-stores {'\"'unrelated {'ghost 42}}\"'` to compare with the old union rule. The actual vector is:\n\n```clojure\n[[:const 1 false] [:branch-false 1 4] [:var 0 ghost] [:jump 7]\n [:stream-make 3 4] [:stream-cursor 2 3] [:stream-next 0 2] [:halt 0]]\n```\n\nThe carried wait is an `:act` at PC 7, with empty E, window and K. Nothing can return to PC 2; no carried closure retains a route to it. Current lift nevertheless refuses with `:yin.k/unsatisfied`, discovery `:incomplete`, obligations `#{ghost}`. Feeding the source stream makes the original machine complete with **73**.\n\nWith only the union-discharge mutant enabled, the same state lifts and lowers, carries **zero module stores**, attaches one stream, and both source and receiver complete with **73**. Thus this is a valid state accepted by the old rule and falsely refused by the new context rule; the unrelated store is not needed by execution or transport. The fix report'\"'s context-precision concern is reproduced, not hypothetical.\n\nRequired change: determine obligations from code reachable from each context's PC, including nested closures that can still be constructed. Use control-flow reachability rather than merely filtering occurrence PCs numerically: backward jumps can make earlier code live. Keep the new per-E/per-active-store answering rule. Add a resumed-PC regression that proves successful transport and completion, and retain negative coverage for genuinely reachable missing names.\n\n### F7 — High: lower accepts a missing module store named by a carried closure\n\n\"'`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1621–1640`\n\nLower checks code/tree coverage, closure origin/binders and primitives, but never checks that each closure or frame `store-of` names a module-store row in its own task. This violates continuation design §2.6: “every `store-of` resolves to a carried module store.” This is a remaining validation gap, rather than a newly introduced line of code.\n\nIndependent reproduction:\n\n1. Run `(fn [] ghost)` to obtain a real closure.\n2. Route its E to `scoped`, with `:module-stores {'\"'scoped {'ghost 42}}\"'`.\n3. Put an authentic stream reference in the root store, so successful attachment is observable.\n4. Lift normally, decode, remove the task'\"'s module-store rows with \"'`(assoc-in task [:slots 7] [])`, and **re-emit** with `rows/emit`.\n5. Lower into a fresh receiver, store its result as `f`, and run `(f)`.\n\nThe original carrier attaches once and `(f)` returns **42**. The edited carrier also **accepts and attaches once**, but `(f)` throws `Unable to resolve symbol: ghost in this context`, ex-data `{:symbol ghost}`. Row hashes, canonical numbering, code and tree associations are valid; the omitted store is the defect.\n\nRequired change: before composition/attachment, validate every non-nil `store-of` against the module-store census of that task, including closures nested in values, acts, return frames, parked/reified states, and install children. A child cannot use a sibling'\"'s or parent's store merely because it has the same key. Add zero-attachment regressions for missing stores, with positive coverage for present empty stores and valid scoped stores.\n\n## Original findings F1–F5\n\nThe original recipes were rerun independently using public harness fixtures, editing decoded tasks and re-emitting with \"'`rows/emit` rather than bypassing hashes. Logs and executable probes are under `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix1-*`.\n\n| Finding | Independent result |\n|---|---|\n| F1 root store `yin/def → [:lit 42]` | `:yin.k/undecodable :reserved-name`, role `:store-key`, **0 attaches**. |\n| F1 carried closure entry `999999999` | `:yin.k/undecodable :closure-origin`, **0 attaches**. The suite also checks module-store reserved keys and binder mismatch. |\n| F2 row 10 response `{}` | `:yin.k/undecodable :install-response`, **0 attaches**. |\n| F2 row 10 phase `:bogus` | `:yin.k/undecodable :install-phase`, **0 attaches**. |\n| F3 `ghost` closure, alone or with unrelated module store | Both refuse `:yin.k/unsatisfied`, discovery `:incomplete`, obligations `#{ghost}`. The valid routed-store reproduction transports and returns 42. |\n| F4 missing trees plus custody | Reports missing trees as `:yin.k/unsatisfied`, **0 attaches**, before custody. Cross-tree ordering tests also pass. |\n| F5 redundant attachment and premature discovery | Independent duplicate-attach mutant now fails **25 assertions**; discharge-before-walk now fails **7 assertions**. Both survived the previous quick/slow harness. |\n\nF1–F5 are therefore resolved for their stated reproductions. F6 does not justify restoring the union rule; F7 is additional carried-state validation.\n\n## Staging, attachment preflight and install review\n\n`decode` retains gate → structure/graph → hashes → canonical form → numbering → frames. Lower then runs stores, pendings/install records, dependencies and custody **one stage at a time across the whole task tree**, before `attach-streams!`. Root custody cannot mask a child'\"'s dependency failure, and a root dependency failure cannot mask a child's pending/install failure. The original store/closure/install refusals now have named transport outcomes before stream attachment. F7 shows that this does not yet establish complete carrier validation.\n\n\"'`attaches?` calls `module/attach-module` and discards the returned VM. On this semantic-register implementation it is pure: `attach-image` validates/analyses the vector and builds persistent code/alias maps; it runs no evaluator, creates no stream handle, calls no composition attacher or secret source, and changes no mutable receiver field. I independently lowered row 10 into a receiver already holding its carried vectors; it accepted and the original receiver value remained equal to its saved value. I found no valid-vector false refusal in that case.\n\nThe vector is validated/analysed in preflight and again during restoration. This duplicates CPU work, but does not duplicate stream attachment or observable execution. Install response images are likewise preflighted and loaded later in a fresh child. No side-effect or double-counting defect was established in these paths. Stream-attachment counts deliberately measure the composition'\"'s \"'`attach!`, not pure code-map construction.\n\nInstall validation accepts the existing blocked-child records, including the corpus'\"'s phase-\"'`:running` synthetic case and tail-delivery case. Phase, response status/name, export shape, image admissibility and child kind are checked before composition. I did not establish an additional valid install state refused by these checks. The blocked-child restriction matches the current engine'\"'s \"'`advance-install`: blocked children are retained, halted children proceed to linking in that sweep. The documented executable parked-child scenario remains an architectural follow-up from the previous review.\n\n## Per-context discharge assessment\n\nContexts are collected for closures, acts and return frames, including values reached transitively through E and module stores. Tail states contribute their K contexts and deliberately contribute no running context for their own dead segment. Nested bodies are associated by the owner chain. The answering order now short-circuits E → the one active store → primitive → installing export → registry. Root-store keys no longer answer a routed closure when its module store lacks the name.\n\nIndependent extra probes show a freely used host-class primitive refuses with `:host-state-primitive`, while the same name shadowed by the closure'\"'s E or by its active store lifts successfully. This confirms the new ordering rather than the previous unconditional host-profile check. Existing tests exercise closure-only, transitive E/store, and routed-store obligations; corpus transport exercises act, return-frame and tail contexts. The remaining context reachability problem is F6. Missing store-census validation is F7. Receiver registry prerequisites remain the previously accepted composition prerequisite; this review does not reclassify them as a new carrier slot or require registry transport.\n\n## Independent mutations\n\nEach mutation was applied separately. After **every** run, the target was restored using \"'`cp`, compared with its backup using `diff -q`, and checked with `git diff --quiet -- <target>`. Both source and harness match HEAD at the end. Backups: `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix1-rows.backup` and `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix1-harness.backup`. All tests ran in foreground subprocesses, sequentially.\n\n| Mutant | Concrete change | Quick harness result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Drop stores stage | Remove `check-stores!` from the staged loop. | Killed: **4 failures**. |\n| Drop pendings stage | Remove `check-pendings!` from the loop. | Killed: **11 failures**. |\n| Drop dependencies stage | Remove `check-dependencies!` from the loop. | Killed: **12 failures**. |\n| Drop custody stage | Remove `check-custody!` from the loop. | Killed: **2 failures**. |\n| Restore union discharge | Answer names from root or any module store, regardless of context. | Killed: **3 failures**. Separately admits F6'\"'s valid state. |\n| Skip install-phase check | Disable the lower-side phase guard. | Killed: **3 failures**. |\n| Custody first | Move custody to the front of the staged loop. | Killed: **4 failures**. |\n| Attach before closure check | Remove closure preflight so restore's \"'`lower-closure` detects the defect after stream attachment. | Killed: **4 failures**. |\n| Make attach-count law always pass | Replace `laws`'\"' count equality assertion with \"'`(is true ...)`. | **Survives** quick **439 assertions**, and slow matrix **698 assertions**, all passing. |\n| Duplicate attachment | Invoke `remote/lower-marker` again when an identity is already present. | Killed: **25 failures**. |\n| Discover before walk (old M9) | Discharge against empty contexts/code before `free-node`; remove later discharge. | Killed: **7 failures**. |\n\nThe always-pass assertion mutant'\"'s survival is a test-deletion survivor: independent assertions do not require the general law to execute. It does **not** make the actual law vacuous. Expected counts come from the wire's distinct stream identities; actual counts increment inside the real composition attacher and are captured immediately after lower. The duplicate-attach mutant changes only production behavior and is detected. The explicit shared-tree test also verifies repeated occurrence count and identical restored handles. No other requested production mutant survived.\n\n## Portability read\n\nThe new code uses persistent maps/sets, atoms, protocol dispatch, portable sequence/transducer operations, and reader-conditional exception types (\"'`Throwable` / `:default` / `Object`). No JVM-only reflection, private-var call, numeric host-class dependency, unary-minus call, or new multi-key assoc into a potentially nil value was found. `task-tree`/`rows-of` traverse bounded carrier data; code bodies come from validated vectors. `attaches?` uses the same portable loader protocol as actual restoration.\n\nThe pinned ClojureDart core (`7d00ee2a…`) defines `fn?` for Dart Function/Fn, `coll?` via ICollection, and supplies `contains?`, `mapcat` and `run!`; the new usages match those contracts. Node and Dart execution was **not run**. This is a portability source review, not evidence that the three-host execution gate passed.\n\n## Verification and artifacts\n\n- `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -n yin.vm.store-write-audit-test`: **49 tests, 1,021 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n- Same command with `-i :slow`: **2 tests, 706 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n- Original reproductions: `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix1-probes.clj` and `.log`.\n- F6/F7 and resolve-order probes: `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix1-extra.clj`, `.log`, and `review-6b-fix1-union-valid-state.log` (the latter proves both transported completions are 73).\n- Mutation scripts/logs: `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix1-mutants.py`, `review-6b-fix1-more-mutants.py`, and per-mutant `.log` files.\n- Final `diff -q` against both backups and `git diff --quiet` on both tracked targets pass. No commits, merges or implementation fixes were made.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED\nEOF'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_67","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat > collab/1792300000000-review-6b-fix1.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md <<'EOF'\n# Slice 6b fix round 1 — independent re-review\n\nReviewed \"'`srvm-phase6b`, HEAD `a396f99699992e8dd0d17d45124054617e68c407`, against the previous findings, the fix report, and continuation design §§2.6, 3.3 and 7.2. The original F1–F5 cases are fixed, and the requested suites pass. Two additional dependency defects remain. No implementation or test edits remain; this findings file is the only permanent review addition.\n\n## Findings\n\n### F6 — Medium: discharge checks code that cannot run from the carried context\n\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1420–1426`\n\nThe context records its resume PC, but discharge uses that PC only to select a body. It then checks every free occurrence in that body and every enclosed body, including occurrences that are behind the resume PC and cannot be reached again. This can reject a valid continuation.\n\nIndependent evaluator-produced reproduction:\n\n```clojure\n{:type :if\n :test (s/lit false)\n :consequent (s/v '\"'ghost)\n :alternate (s/next-of (s/cursor-of {:type :stream/make :buffer 4}))}\n\"'```\n\nRun this to block, then add `:module-stores {'\"'unrelated {'ghost 42}}\"'` to compare with the old union rule. The actual vector is:\n\n```clojure\n[[:const 1 false] [:branch-false 1 4] [:var 0 ghost] [:jump 7]\n [:stream-make 3 4] [:stream-cursor 2 3] [:stream-next 0 2] [:halt 0]]\n```\n\nThe carried wait is an `:act` at PC 7, with empty E, window and K. Nothing can return to PC 2; no carried closure retains a route to it. Current lift nevertheless refuses with `:yin.k/unsatisfied`, discovery `:incomplete`, obligations `#{ghost}`. Feeding the source stream makes the original machine complete with **73**.\n\nWith only the union-discharge mutant enabled, the same state lifts and lowers, carries **zero module stores**, attaches one stream, and both source and receiver complete with **73**. Thus this is a valid state accepted by the old rule and falsely refused by the new context rule; the unrelated store is not needed by execution or transport. The fix report'\"'s context-precision concern is reproduced, not hypothetical.\n\nRequired change: determine obligations from code reachable from each context's PC, including nested closures that can still be constructed. Use control-flow reachability rather than merely filtering occurrence PCs numerically: backward jumps can make earlier code live. Keep the new per-E/per-active-store answering rule. Add a resumed-PC regression that proves successful transport and completion, and retain negative coverage for genuinely reachable missing names.\n\n### F7 — High: lower accepts a missing module store named by a carried closure\n\n\"'`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1621–1640`\n\nLower checks code/tree coverage, closure origin/binders and primitives, but never checks that each closure or frame `store-of` names a module-store row in its own task. This violates continuation design §2.6: “every `store-of` resolves to a carried module store.” This is a remaining validation gap, rather than a newly introduced line of code.\n\nIndependent reproduction:\n\n1. Run `(fn [] ghost)` to obtain a real closure.\n2. Route its E to `scoped`, with `:module-stores {'\"'scoped {'ghost 42}}\"'`.\n3. Put an authentic stream reference in the root store, so successful attachment is observable.\n4. Lift normally, decode, remove the task'\"'s module-store rows with \"'`(assoc-in task [:slots 7] [])`, and **re-emit** with `rows/emit`.\n5. Lower into a fresh receiver, store its result as `f`, and run `(f)`.\n\nThe original carrier attaches once and `(f)` returns **42**. The edited carrier also **accepts and attaches once**, but `(f)` throws `Unable to resolve symbol: ghost in this context`, ex-data `{:symbol ghost}`. Row hashes, canonical numbering, code and tree associations are valid; the omitted store is the defect.\n\nRequired change: before composition/attachment, validate every non-nil `store-of` against the module-store census of that task, including closures nested in values, acts, return frames, parked/reified states, and install children. A child cannot use a sibling'\"'s or parent's store merely because it has the same key. Add zero-attachment regressions for missing stores, with positive coverage for present empty stores and valid scoped stores.\n\n## Original findings F1–F5\n\nThe original recipes were rerun independently using public harness fixtures, editing decoded tasks and re-emitting with \"'`rows/emit` rather than bypassing hashes. Logs and executable probes are under `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix1-*`.\n\n| Finding | Independent result |\n|---|---|\n| F1 root store `yin/def → [:lit 42]` | `:yin.k/undecodable :reserved-name`, role `:store-key`, **0 attaches**. |\n| F1 carried closure entry `999999999` | `:yin.k/undecodable :closure-origin`, **0 attaches**. The suite also checks module-store reserved keys and binder mismatch. |\n| F2 row 10 response `{}` | `:yin.k/undecodable :install-response`, **0 attaches**. |\n| F2 row 10 phase `:bogus` | `:yin.k/undecodable :install-phase`, **0 attaches**. |\n| F3 `ghost` closure, alone or with unrelated module store | Both refuse `:yin.k/unsatisfied`, discovery `:incomplete`, obligations `#{ghost}`. The valid routed-store reproduction transports and returns 42. |\n| F4 missing trees plus custody | Reports missing trees as `:yin.k/unsatisfied`, **0 attaches**, before custody. Cross-tree ordering tests also pass. |\n| F5 redundant attachment and premature discovery | Independent duplicate-attach mutant now fails **25 assertions**; discharge-before-walk now fails **7 assertions**. Both survived the previous quick/slow harness. |\n\nF1–F5 are therefore resolved for their stated reproductions. F6 does not justify restoring the union rule; F7 is additional carried-state validation.\n\n## Staging, attachment preflight and install review\n\n`decode` retains gate → structure/graph → hashes → canonical form → numbering → frames. Lower then runs stores, pendings/install records, dependencies and custody **one stage at a time across the whole task tree**, before `attach-streams!`. Root custody cannot mask a child'\"'s dependency failure, and a root dependency failure cannot mask a child's pending/install failure. The original store/closure/install refusals now have named transport outcomes before stream attachment. F7 shows that this does not yet establish complete carrier validation.\n\n\"'`attaches?` calls `module/attach-module` and discards the returned VM. On this semantic-register implementation it is pure: `attach-image` validates/analyses the vector and builds persistent code/alias maps; it runs no evaluator, creates no stream handle, calls no composition attacher or secret source, and changes no mutable receiver field. I independently lowered row 10 into a receiver already holding its carried vectors; it accepted and the original receiver value remained equal to its saved value. I found no valid-vector false refusal in that case.\n\nThe vector is validated/analysed in preflight and again during restoration. This duplicates CPU work, but does not duplicate stream attachment or observable execution. Install response images are likewise preflighted and loaded later in a fresh child. No side-effect or double-counting defect was established in these paths. Stream-attachment counts deliberately measure the composition'\"'s \"'`attach!`, not pure code-map construction.\n\nInstall validation accepts the existing blocked-child records, including the corpus'\"'s phase-\"'`:running` synthetic case and tail-delivery case. Phase, response status/name, export shape, image admissibility and child kind are checked before composition. I did not establish an additional valid install state refused by these checks. The blocked-child restriction matches the current engine'\"'s \"'`advance-install`: blocked children are retained, halted children proceed to linking in that sweep. The documented executable parked-child scenario remains an architectural follow-up from the previous review.\n\n## Per-context discharge assessment\n\nContexts are collected for closures, acts and return frames, including values reached transitively through E and module stores. Tail states contribute their K contexts and deliberately contribute no running context for their own dead segment. Nested bodies are associated by the owner chain. The answering order now short-circuits E → the one active store → primitive → installing export → registry. Root-store keys no longer answer a routed closure when its module store lacks the name.\n\nIndependent extra probes show a freely used host-class primitive refuses with `:host-state-primitive`, while the same name shadowed by the closure'\"'s E or by its active store lifts successfully. This confirms the new ordering rather than the previous unconditional host-profile check. Existing tests exercise closure-only, transitive E/store, and routed-store obligations; corpus transport exercises act, return-frame and tail contexts. The remaining context reachability problem is F6. Missing store-census validation is F7. Receiver registry prerequisites remain the previously accepted composition prerequisite; this review does not reclassify them as a new carrier slot or require registry transport.\n\n## Independent mutations\n\nEach mutation was applied separately. After **every** run, the target was restored using \"'`cp`, compared with its backup using `diff -q`, and checked with `git diff --quiet -- <target>`. Both source and harness match HEAD at the end. Backups: `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix1-rows.backup` and `/private/tmp/review-6b-fix1-harness.backup`. All tests ran in foreground subprocesses, sequentially.\n\n| Mutant | Concrete change | Quick harness result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Drop stores stage | Remove `check-stores!` from the staged loop. | Killed: *